As vector databases scale in Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG), the retrieval phase increasingly bottlenecks end-to-end latency. While Compute Express Link (CXL) offers scalable memory expansion, naïve CXL deployments suffer from intra-device bandwidth saturation and inter-device load imbalance, which collectively hinder system responsiveness. We propose Clone, a hardware-software co-designed system that leverages computational CXL memory to accelerate approximate nearest neighbor search. Clone features a rank-level processing unit with a µ-batch execution model, exploiting RAG query concurrency to coalesce redundant memory accesses and maximize internal bandwidth. At the system level, an adjacency-aware index placement mitigates access skew, while a runtime early-generation technique decouples the RAG workflow from device tail latency. Evaluations demonstrate that Clone achieves up to 31 × and 33.8 × throughput gains on IVF and HNSW over CXL baselines, reducing end-to-end RAG latency by up to 65%.